From: Prabhsimran Singh <prabhsimrans@nvidia.com>
Subject: llama: size the batched serving output buffers by what is read

Batched serving samples only token ids on the backend, so its pinned output
buffer no longer holds full-vocabulary logits, probabilities and candidates for
every possible output row (several GB with hundreds of sequences and MTP verify
rows). The raw logits rows are reserved once a decode first returns raw logits
to the host (an output row of a sequence without a backend sampler; chained MTP
drafts never do), and device-resident NextN rows get a host copy only when
they are downloaded. A batched context gives memory back when its reserved
size drops.

Upstream: not planned (batched serving path).

